So, this is the point in the conversation
where you ask me if l'd like a ride home.

480
00:34:11,945 --> 00:34:14,095
- lt is?
- Yep.

481
00:34:16,625 --> 00:34:20,379
Would you like a ride home?

482
00:34:20,465 --> 00:34:23,935
Fine... but l'm not getting in that sidecar.

483
00:34:24,025 --> 00:34:26,539
- Why not?
- Sidecars are for bitches.

484
00:34:26,625 --> 00:34:29,344
Anyone who rides in that
is automatically your bitch.

485
00:34:29,425 --> 00:34:31,381
Thus, l better ride on the back.

486
00:34:39,825 --> 00:34:41,736
- Oh.
- Hold on tight.

487
00:34:41,825 --> 00:34:43,736
OK, holdin'!

488
00:34:47,025 --> 00:34:49,414
My hair's blowin' in the wind.

489
00:34:53,865 --> 00:34:55,742
What, are you, like, a hand glider?

490
00:34:55,825 --> 00:34:59,500
My boyfriend was gonna pick me up,
so l brought it, but then he couldn't.

491
00:34:59,585 --> 00:35:01,735
What does he have,
one of those tandem things?

492
00:35:01,825 --> 00:35:04,623
No, he drives a Ninja.
Goes way faster than this guy.

493
00:35:04,705 --> 00:35:07,822
- What kind of helmet is that?
- You can wear it on a bike.

494
00:35:07,905 --> 00:35:10,135
- How's your head?
- l should live through the day.

495
00:35:10,225 --> 00:35:12,261
- Cool. Make a right here.
- All right.

496
00:35:17,705 --> 00:35:21,300
- Are you doing anything right now?
- Can you elaborate on ''doing anything''?

497
00:35:21,385 --> 00:35:24,616
lt's just, l know this guy, Jesse.
He bought this mansion right up here.

498
00:35:24,705 --> 00:35:28,095
And he wants me to come visit him,
but l don't want to stay very long.

499
00:35:28,185 --> 00:35:30,699
So, l was thinking, like, if you came, too,

500
00:35:30,785 --> 00:35:33,299
l could just say l have to take you home
when l'm ready to go.

501
00:35:33,385 --> 00:35:37,219
Wow. That's pretty damn
random of you, Andrew.

502
00:35:37,305 --> 00:35:39,694
- Well...
- ''Nice to meet you. Can l use you?''

503
00:35:39,785 --> 00:35:42,618
- lt's the Hollywood in you.
- No. Come on, it's not like that.

504
00:35:42,705 --> 00:35:47,096
lt'll be fun, come on. l'll tell you what.
We can have, like, a signal.

505
00:35:47,185 --> 00:35:50,143
Like, when you pull on your ear,
that's the code.

506
00:35:50,225 --> 00:35:52,216
And l'll be like,
''Oh, l got to take her home.''

507
00:35:52,305 --> 00:35:55,456
And then, then we'll go.

508
00:35:55,545 --> 00:35:57,536
Can we have code names, too?

509
00:35:57,625 --> 00:36:00,185
lf you want.

510
00:36:00,265 --> 00:36:03,223
OK... but don't try and, like,
kidnap me or anything,

511
00:36:03,305 --> 00:36:07,093
'cause my step uncle's a bounty hunter,
and he could have you tracked and killed.

512
00:36:07,185 --> 00:36:09,141
You're such a liar.

513
00:36:11,585 --> 00:36:13,496
Whoo!

514
00:36:14,745 --> 00:36:17,020
OK, you ready?

515
00:36:37,065 --> 00:36:40,023
l'll be fine. Am l the first boy
you've ever brought home?

516
00:36:40,105 --> 00:36:44,940
No, but, um... l lied to you before when l
told you that my boyfriend drives a Ninja.

517
00:36:45,025 --> 00:36:49,018
- He doesn't drive a bike?
- No, l don't have a boyfriend.

518
00:36:49,105 --> 00:36:52,984
But, you know, he might drive a Ninja...
wherever he is.

519
00:36:53,065 --> 00:36:54,942
Wow, we're off to a great start.

520
00:36:55,025 --> 00:36:59,143
OK. So-So sometimes l lie.

521
00:36:59,225 --> 00:37:01,659
l mean, l'm weird, man.

522
00:37:01,745 --> 00:37:04,942
About random stuff, too.
l don't even know why l do it.

523
00:37:05,025 --> 00:37:08,859
lt's like, it's like a tic. l mean, sometimes
l hear myself say something,

524
00:37:08,945 --> 00:37:13,461
and then l think,
''Wow, that wasn't even remotely true.''

525
00:37:13,545 --> 00:37:16,013
So, how do people know what's real?

526
00:37:16,105 --> 00:37:21,463
Well, l always feel bad afterwards
and admit them when they're lies.

527
00:37:21,545 --> 00:37:26,061
- Can you trust that?
- Open the door.

528
00:37:33,945 --> 00:37:36,015
Kevin, down. Mom!

529
00:37:36,105 --> 00:37:40,178
Come on, kick 'em in the balls.
Come on. Mom!

530
00:37:41,185 --> 00:37:43,938
Everybody down.
Who wants to eat?

531
00:37:44,025 --> 00:37:46,175
Do you want to eat?
Then get the fuck off him.

532
00:37:46,265 --> 00:37:48,142
Go on, get out.

533
00:37:50,305 --> 00:37:52,978
l'm so sorry.
We just don't have the time to train them.

534
00:37:53,065 --> 00:37:55,260
- Who's got the time to train them?
- Mom.

535
00:37:55,345 --> 00:37:58,337
- This is Andrew.
- Welcome, welcome.

536
00:37:58,425 --> 00:38:00,814
- l'm sorry the place is a wreck. Honey?
- Mm-hmm?

537
00:38:00,905 --> 00:38:03,419
l asked you to get the metal wheel
out of the hamster cage?

538
00:38:03,505 --> 00:38:06,065
- l forgot.
- Well, you forgot, and now Jelly's dead.

539
00:38:06,145 --> 00:38:08,340
Luckily, l got Peanut Butter out in time.

540
00:38:08,425 --> 00:38:10,336
We have to get the only hamsters
on planet Earth

541
00:38:10,425 --> 00:38:12,814
who can't figure out
a stupid hamster wheel.

542
00:38:12,905 --> 00:38:14,941
There's a tin box on the kitchen counter.

543
00:38:15,025 --> 00:38:17,414
You can do the honors.
l have to get to work.

544
00:38:17,505 --> 00:38:21,020
- lt's nice to meet you. Andrew, right?
- Yes. Very nice to meet you.

545
00:38:21,105 --> 00:38:24,017
Samantha, put the clothes
in the dryer and bury Jelly.

546
00:38:29,265 --> 00:38:31,221
So, this is it.

547
00:38:38,425 --> 00:38:40,893
- lt's nice.
- No, it's not.

548
00:38:40,985 --> 00:38:43,260
Don't say that 'cause
you can't think of anything else.

549
00:38:43,345 --> 00:38:48,021
l'm not, l'm not. lt's very cozy. You guys
are a little early on the tree, though.

550
00:38:48,105 --> 00:38:50,539
Yeah, well, we never got around
to taking it down,

551
00:38:50,625 --> 00:38:53,583
so when it got to be fall again,
we figured, just leave it up.

552
00:38:53,665 --> 00:38:56,941
Oh, my God.
You're totally freaked out right now.

553
00:38:57,025 --> 00:39:01,496
- You, like, can't wait to leave.
- l'm not, l'm not. l like it. lt's very nice.

554
00:39:01,585 --> 00:39:04,304
- You want to see my room?
- Sure.

555
00:39:06,825 --> 00:39:10,613
Hey. Titembay, this is my friend Andrew.
Andrew, this is my brother, Titembay.

556
00:39:10,705 --> 00:39:14,061
- Hi, it's very nice to meet you.
- Oh, it's nice to meet you.

557
00:39:14,145 --> 00:39:16,056
- l'm sorry about Jelly.
- Oh, that's OK.

558
00:39:16,145 --> 00:39:18,500
l saved some mac and cheese for you.
lt's in the fridge.

559
00:39:18,585 --> 00:39:20,940
Thank you.

560
00:39:24,545 --> 00:39:27,013
Anyway... l'm late for class.

561
00:39:27,105 --> 00:39:29,380
- lt's nice meeting you.
- Very nice to meet you.

562
00:39:29,465 --> 00:39:32,184
See you later.

563
00:39:34,985 --> 00:39:38,261
So, here we are.
lt's nothing fancy, but you know...

564
00:39:38,345 --> 00:39:40,620
What?

565
00:39:40,705 --> 00:39:43,173
Uh, it's Titembay, right?

566
00:39:43,265 --> 00:39:46,257
Yeah, it's weird, huh?
You're, like, so freaked out right now.

567
00:39:46,345 --> 00:39:49,178
You're running for the door. lt's OK.
You can go. Don't feel bad--

568
00:39:49,265 --> 00:39:50,698
- Stop doing that.
- What?

569
00:39:50,785 --> 00:39:54,300
The thing you just did. Don't do that.
l want to be here. lf l didn't, l wouldn't be.

570
00:39:54,385 --> 00:39:57,183
Trust me, my family is way more
fucked up than yours, OK?

571
00:39:57,265 --> 00:39:58,857
OK.

572
00:39:58,945 --> 00:40:01,698
So... Titembay.

573
00:40:01,785 --> 00:40:03,935
Yeah, he's my brother.

574
00:40:04,025 --> 00:40:06,619
- ls he adopted, or...?
- Kind of.

575
00:40:06,705 --> 00:40:09,014
My mom adopted him
from Sally Struthers years ago.

576
00:40:09,105 --> 00:40:11,858
One of those ''For the cost
of a cup of coffee a day'' things.

577
00:40:11,945 --> 00:40:14,857
Where she's like, ''How can you
sit there and not help the children?''

578
00:40:14,945 --> 00:40:17,823
And we just couldn't sit there
and not help the children.

579
00:40:17,905 --> 00:40:22,057
So, we start sending him, like,
pictures and letters and stuff for years,

580
00:40:22,145 --> 00:40:25,615
but then l got really into ice skating,
so we sort of forgot about him.

581
00:40:25,705 --> 00:40:28,936
Then one day... we get this phone call,
and it's Titembay.

582
00:40:29,025 --> 00:40:31,664
He's at the dry cleaners
around the corner. And he's like,

583
00:40:31,745 --> 00:40:35,181
''l'm at Rutgers. l live in the dorms,
but l'm used to living with my tribe,

584
00:40:35,265 --> 00:40:39,577
so l'd rather live with a family.''
And he's been living with us ever since.

585
00:40:39,665 --> 00:40:42,020
Wow.

586
00:40:42,105 --> 00:40:45,859
l know. He's the most amazing guy.
You ought to hear some of his stories.

587
00:40:45,945 --> 00:40:48,778
He struggled through so much
because he wanted to learn.

588
00:40:48,865 --> 00:40:52,858
When l think of what he's accomplished,
l just, l just feel, like, lazy, you know?

589
00:40:52,945 --> 00:40:55,175
He's studying criminal justice at Rutgers,

590
00:40:55,265 --> 00:40:58,894
and when he was a baby, he was one of
those kids with the flies all over his face.

591
00:41:00,025 --> 00:41:04,257
Wow.
l mean, it's a crazy story.

592
00:41:04,345 --> 00:41:07,781
Yeah, l know. l mean,
that's a true story. l am not that good.

593
00:41:07,865 --> 00:41:11,380
Ohh... this is Tickle.

594
00:41:11,465 --> 00:41:13,342
What is Tickle?

595
00:41:13,425 --> 00:41:15,814
Tickle's my favorite thing
in the whole world.

596
00:41:15,905 --> 00:41:18,783
lt's all that's left of Nanny, my blanket.

597
00:41:18,865 --> 00:41:20,901
- Tickle's all that remains.
- Mm-hmm.

598
00:41:20,985 --> 00:41:23,340
- Was there, like, a hurricane?
- Shut up!

599
00:41:23,425 --> 00:41:26,622
No, l mean...
l've had this since l was a baby.

600
00:41:26,705 --> 00:41:30,618
lt's what they brought me
home in from the hospital.

601
00:41:30,705 --> 00:41:33,424
- lt's like the Wailing Wall.
- What?

602
00:41:33,505 --> 00:41:36,178
Oh, the Wailing Wall is, like, this...

603
00:41:36,265 --> 00:41:39,257
lt's, like, the most holy place for Jews
to go and pray in lsrael.

604
00:41:39,345 --> 00:41:44,055
lt's all that's left of this enormous temple
that was destroyed by the Romans.

605
00:41:44,145 --> 00:41:47,057
- So you're, like, really Jewish.
- What?

606
00:41:47,145 --> 00:41:49,705
You are, aren't you? You are.

607
00:41:49,785 --> 00:41:52,219
l'm not. l mean, l'm Jewish,
but l'm not really Jewish.

608
00:41:52,305 --> 00:41:54,216
l mean, l don't do anything Jewish.

609
00:41:54,305 --> 00:41:57,536
l don't go to temple or anything, but l
don't know any Jews that go to temple.

610
00:41:57,625 --> 00:42:01,334
The Jews l know, they go on one day.
lt's Yom Kippur, the Day of Repentance.

611
00:42:01,425 --> 00:42:02,858
OK.

612
00:42:02,945 --> 00:42:05,903
Did you know that most temples
are built with moveable walls,

613
00:42:05,985 --> 00:42:08,545
so that on the one day of the year
when everyone comes to repent,

614
00:42:08,625 --> 00:42:11,378
they can actually make
the room big enough to hold everyone?

615
00:42:11,465 --> 00:42:14,263
l don't really believe in God.

616
00:42:14,345 --> 00:42:16,859
- Just Tickle.
- Oh, l believe in Tickle.

617
00:42:19,105 --> 00:42:22,142
- We're not gonna make out or anything.
- What?

618
00:42:22,225 --> 00:42:24,898
l'm sorry. l just totally
ruined that moment, didn't l?

619
00:42:24,985 --> 00:42:28,295
- lt's just, we're not gonna make out, OK?
- No, l hadn't planned on trying--

620
00:42:28,385 --> 00:42:30,979
Oh, l am so lame.
That was, like, really--

621
00:42:31,065 --> 00:42:34,819
l'm... l'm sorry. Forget l just said that.
l'm-- That was dumb.

622
00:42:34,905 --> 00:42:37,817
You know what l do when l feel
completely unoriginal?

623
00:42:37,905 --> 00:42:38,894
What?

624
00:42:39,985 --> 00:42:43,580
Blah, blah, blah, blah, la!

625
00:42:43,665 --> 00:42:46,816
l-l make a noise, or l do something
that no one has ever done before,

626
00:42:46,905 --> 00:42:50,580
and then l can feel unique again,
even if it's only for, like, a second.

627
00:42:50,665 --> 00:42:54,101
- So, no one's ever done that?
- No. Not in this spot, no.

628
00:42:54,185 --> 00:42:57,575
You just witnessed a completely
original moment in human history.

629
00:42:57,665 --> 00:42:59,701
- lt's refreshing. You should try it.
- No, thanks.

630
00:42:59,785 --> 00:43:01,821
- Come on.
- No, l think that was good enough

631
00:43:01,905 --> 00:43:04,624
- for the both of us.
- Come on! What are you, shy?

632
00:43:04,705 --> 00:43:08,823
This is your one opportunity to do
something no one has ever done before,

633
00:43:08,905 --> 00:43:11,260
that no one will copy again
throughout human existence.

634
00:43:11,345 --> 00:43:13,495
And if nothing else,
you'll be remembered

635
00:43:13,585 --> 00:43:17,578
as the one guy who ever did this -
this one thing.

636
00:43:19,545 --> 00:43:21,979
Uh...

637
00:43:27,505 --> 00:43:30,622
- How was that?
- Oh, l've done that one before.

638
00:43:31,945 --> 00:43:35,904
So, l got to go bury this hamster
before the dogs eat him.

639
00:43:35,985 --> 00:43:38,579
Wanna help? Hmm?

640
00:43:42,945 --> 00:43:45,584
Stood beneath an orange sky

641
00:43:49,305 --> 00:43:52,581
- Wow.
- Yeah.

642
00:43:52,665 --> 00:43:54,815
- l mean, this is, uh--
- l know.

643
00:43:55,825 --> 00:43:57,816
lt's not that we're bad pet owners.

644
00:43:57,905 --> 00:44:01,614
lt's just, you know, we've had
so many of them over the years.

645
00:44:01,705 --> 00:44:03,935
Besides, a lot of these are fish.

646
00:44:04,025 --> 00:44:06,414
After you.

647
00:44:13,785 --> 00:44:16,458
All right.

648
00:44:18,785 --> 00:44:20,696
Sit down.

649
00:44:27,785 --> 00:44:29,696
What are you thinking about?

650
00:44:30,945 --> 00:44:33,334
- Now?
- Yeah.

651
00:44:33,425 --> 00:44:36,656
- Right now?
- Yeah.

652
00:44:36,745 --> 00:44:39,976
Right now, l was thinking l've been going
to a lot of these things lately.

653
00:44:40,065 --> 00:44:42,533
- What, dates?
- Not dates.

654
00:44:42,625 --> 00:44:45,503
This is a date?
This isn't a date.

655
00:44:45,585 --> 00:44:47,576
Funerals.

656
00:44:47,665 --> 00:44:49,576
Why? Who else died?

657
00:44:51,185 --> 00:44:53,096
Oh, um...

658
00:44:54,025 --> 00:44:57,301
That's why l'm home, actually.
l guess l haven't even told you that yet.

659
00:45:01,105 --> 00:45:03,380
My mom just died.

660
00:45:08,065 --> 00:45:10,738
God, that's weird
to say that out loud, but, uh...

661
00:45:12,505 --> 00:45:17,659
My mom just died, and...
yeah, that's why l'm home.

662
00:45:18,545 --> 00:45:21,981
l'm so sorry.
God, l'm so sorry.

663
00:45:22,065 --> 00:45:24,704
And here l am, putting you
through another one of these.

664
00:45:24,785 --> 00:45:28,539
Not that Jelly's anywhere near your mom.
l mean, we loved Jelly, but...

665
00:45:30,345 --> 00:45:33,974
- l'm so sorry.
- No, it's OK. lt's all right.

666
00:45:34,065 --> 00:45:35,976
l'm all right with it.

667
00:45:36,065 --> 00:45:38,784
Anyway, l think it's
what she wanted, so...

668
00:45:38,865 --> 00:45:41,174
How did she die?

669
00:45:42,745 --> 00:45:44,656
She drowned, actually.

670
00:45:47,185 --> 00:45:49,096
Yeah, my mother was a paraplegic.

671
00:45:49,185 --> 00:45:52,814
She was in a wheelchair,
so she... you know.

672
00:45:52,905 --> 00:45:58,423
And... l guess she was taking a bath,
and she must have slipped or something.

673
00:45:58,505 --> 00:46:00,382
That's what they say.
l don't know.

674
00:46:00,465 --> 00:46:02,774
But, needless to say, she drowned,

675
00:46:02,865 --> 00:46:06,540
and that's how she... she died.

676
00:46:06,625 --> 00:46:08,934
When?

677
00:46:09,025 --> 00:46:11,175
This was, uh...

678
00:46:12,305 --> 00:46:14,182
...Sunday.

679
00:46:14,265 --> 00:46:17,143
Oh, my God.

680
00:46:21,825 --> 00:46:23,702
Why are you crying?

681
00:46:25,545 --> 00:46:27,456
l don't know. l'm sorry.

682
00:46:28,785 --> 00:46:31,982
l'm not usually like this.
lt's just, like...

683
00:46:33,465 --> 00:46:35,376
lt's just so sad.

684
00:46:35,465 --> 00:46:37,615
You know, it's like...

685
00:46:37,705 --> 00:46:39,582
lt's so tragic, isn't it? lt is.

686
00:46:39,665 --> 00:46:44,500
l mean, it's like, it's like real-life tragedy
or something. l mean...

687
00:46:44,585 --> 00:46:47,418
Anyway, let's just,
let's change the subject, OK?

688
00:46:47,505 --> 00:46:49,461
Let's really bring
the focus back to Jelly,

689
00:46:49,545 --> 00:46:52,537
'cause what could be ruder than talking
about someone else who died

690
00:46:52,625 --> 00:46:55,264
when you're in the act
of burying a close friend?

691
00:46:56,425 --> 00:46:58,336
- Well, what should we do?
- l don't know.

692
00:46:58,425 --> 00:47:01,542
l've only been to one of these things.
You appear to be the expert.

693
00:47:01,625 --> 00:47:04,344
Well, we usually say something.

694
00:47:04,425 --> 00:47:07,303
OK, l'll go first. Um...

695
00:47:09,105 --> 00:47:12,142
Didn't really know you, Jelly.
From what l hear, you were a good pet.

696
00:47:12,225 --> 00:47:14,455
- Little trouble with the wheel--
- That's not funny.

697
00:47:20,105 --> 00:47:22,414
Jelly, you were a great pet.

698
00:47:24,465 --> 00:47:26,854
l'm sorry l forgot to take
the wheel out of your cage.

699
00:47:26,945 --> 00:47:29,254
l'm so, so sorry about that.

700
00:47:30,785 --> 00:47:33,345
Goodbye.

701
00:47:33,425 --> 00:47:35,541
l hope that you liked me.

702
00:47:53,025 --> 00:47:55,095
If I lived

703
00:47:55,185 --> 00:48:01,579
Till I was 1 02

704
